Matt Frisch <matuse73@yahoo.spam.me.not.com> writes:

> Not being the ultimate (or even close) NH Guru, I'm a bit perplexed by
> something I've recently encountered. After playing Archaeologists
> extensively, always lawful, and usually going for the protection racket
> early on, and thus mining the living hell out of the first 4 levels for
> money, I'm familiar with praying while weak/fainting for a full stomach.
> Never been a problem.
> 
> Until I tried a Wizard. I hate Thoth. For some reason, he cheerfully
> ignores any pressing concerns to tell me that he's happy with me, whereupon
> I die 2 turns later. I've had *dozens* of wizards starve to death because
> prayers were ignored. I've had low HP wizards (low as in 1/50) get a
> cheerful pat on the back instead of a heal. It's actually a momentous
> occasion when a prayer is actually answered, and this is far out of
> character from my previous experiences.
> 
> Is it because my wizards are neutral? Are lawful gods just...better? WTF is
> up with Thoth that he doesn't bother to actually help where other gods seem
> to have no problem with it.

Unlike most classes, wizards start with alignment 0.  Until this is
increased, your god will not reliably answer your prayers.  If Thoth
is telling you that he is "satisfied" rather than "pleased" or
"well-pleased", that is a sign of low alignment.

By far the easiest way to raise alignment is to kill hostile monsters,
but if you're doing the protection racket with your wizards, this
won't be happening.  Your neutrality may be a slight problem - IIRC,
you may not get an alignment boost for killing monsters of your own
alignment.  Many low-level monsters are neutral, though goblins and
kobolds are chaotic.  (Hobbits are lawful, but liable to be peaceful
towards a lawful character.)
